## 15. Navigation Design

Navigation design shows how screens/pages connect.

### Example

```text
Main Menu
├── Add Record
│   └── Save / Clear / Back
├── Search Records
│   └── View Results / Back
├── Weekly Summary
│   └── Print or Back
└── Edit/Delete Record
    └── Save Changes / Delete / Back
```

### Why Useful?

Navigation design shows whether the system will be easy to use and complete.

---

## 16. Input / Output Design

Input/output design explains what data goes into and out of the system.

### Example Table

| Feature | Input | Processing | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| Add record | name, task, date, status | validate and save | confirmation message |
| Search records | student name | compare with stored names | matching records |
| Weekly summary | selected week | count missing tasks | summary table |
| Edit record | selected record + new values | validate and update | updated record |
| Delete record | selected record | remove from storage | deletion confirmation |

### Why Useful?

It connects user actions to data processing.

---

## 17. Data Design

Data design explains how information will be stored.

### Possible Data Structures

```text
list of records
array of objects
dictionary/map
CSV file
JSON file
database table
ArrayList of class objects
```

### Example Record Structure

```text
HomeworkRecord
- studentName
- taskName
- dueDate
- completionStatus
- notes
```

### Example Table Design

| Field | Data Type | Purpose | Validation |
|---|---|---|---|
| recordID | Integer | unique record identifier | auto-generated |
| studentName | String | student name | not blank |
| taskName | String | homework task | not blank |
| dueDate | Date/String | due date | valid date format |
| status | String/Boolean | completed or missing | selected from options |
| notes | String | optional comment | optional |

---

## 18. Algorithm Design

Algorithm design explains important logic.

### Algorithms Could Include

```text
searching records
filtering by date
sorting by score/name/date
calculating totals
generating summaries
validating input
loading and saving data
checking duplicates
updating records
```

### Pseudocode Example: Search

```text
INPUT searchName
results ← empty list

FOR each record IN homeworkRecords
    IF record.studentName = searchName THEN
        ADD record TO results
    ENDIF
ENDFOR

IF length(results) = 0 THEN
    OUTPUT "No matching records found"
ELSE
    OUTPUT results
ENDIF
```

### Explanation

This supports the success criterion:

```text
The teacher can search records by student name and view all matching results.
```

---

## 19. Flowchart Design

Flowcharts are useful for processes with decisions.

### Example: Add Record Validation

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Start Add Record] --> B[User enters record details]
    B --> C{Required fields complete?}
    C -- No --> D[Show error message]
    D --> B
    C -- Yes --> E[Save record]
    E --> F[Show confirmation]
    F --> G[End]
```

### Why Useful?

It shows how the system handles both valid and invalid input.

---

## 20. Validation Design

Validation should be planned before development.

### Common Validation Rules

| Input | Validation Rule | Error Message |
|---|---|---|
| student name | must not be blank | Please enter a student name. |
| task name | must not be blank | Please enter a task name. |
| date | must follow expected format | Please enter a valid date. |
| score | must be between 0 and 100 | Score must be from 0 to 100. |
| quantity | must be a positive integer | Quantity must be greater than 0. |
| status | must be selected from options | Please choose a status. |

### Strong Design Link

Validation criteria should later be tested with invalid data.

---

## 21. Test Planning from Success Criteria

Success criteria should create test ideas.

### Example

| Success Criterion | Test Ideas |
|---|---|
| Add valid record | enter valid record and confirm it appears |
| Reject blank name | leave name blank and check error |
| Search by name | search existing and non-existing names |
| Weekly summary | compare system total with manual total |
| Load saved records | close/reopen system and check records |

### Key Rule

If a success criterion cannot be tested, it is probably too vague.

---

## 22. Design Decisions

Students should explain why they made design choices.

### Examples

```text
I used a table display because the client needs to compare many records quickly.
I used a dropdown for completion status to prevent spelling variations.
I used a local file because the client needs the product to work without internet.
I used a search bar on the main page because the client said searching is the most frequent task.
```

### Weak Design Explanation

```text
I added a button because buttons are good.
```

### Stronger Design Explanation

```text
I added a Save Record button at the bottom of the form so the teacher can check all required fields before submitting the record.
```

---

## 23. Design Change Log

Sometimes the final product changes from the original design. This is normal.

### Good Practice

Record major changes:

| Original Design | Change Made | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| separate search page | search added to records page | client wanted fewer navigation steps |
| free text status input | dropdown status | prevents spelling errors |
| export to PDF | export not included | too large for current project scope |
| manual summary button | automatic summary after filtering | easier for user |

### Why Useful?

It shows reflection and development thinking.

---

## 24. Design Section Structure

A strong design section may include:

```text
1. Design overview
2. Success criteria mapping table
3. User interface wireframes
4. Navigation design
5. Input/output design
6. Data structure or database design
7. Algorithm/pseudocode
8. Validation design
9. Initial test plan
10. Design decisions and justifications
```

### Reminder

Only include design elements that help explain your product. Do not add random diagrams that are not used later.

