## 27. Weak vs Strong Design

| Weak Design | Strong Design |
|---|---|
| I will make a page with buttons. | Main menu has Add, Search, Summary, and Edit options linked to client tasks. |
| I will store data. | Each homework record stores studentName, taskName, dueDate, status, and notes. |
| The system will search. | Search algorithm loops through saved records and displays records where studentName matches input. |
| It will check errors. | Blank required fields will be rejected with clear error messages. |
| It will look simple. | Forms use labelled fields and dropdown status choices to reduce typing errors. |

---

## 28. Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Why It Is Weak | Better Approach |
|---|---|---|
| criteria are vague | cannot test/evaluate | make criteria measurable |
| criteria are just features | weak client link | connect to client needs |
| too many criteria | project too large | focus on core needs |
| design does not mention criteria | weak planning | map design to criteria |
| wireframes only, no data/algorithm design | lacks CS depth | include data and logic design |
| no validation design | invalid testing becomes weak | plan validation rules |
| design written after coding with no changes explained | appears artificial | document design decisions and changes |
| algorithms not explained | technical thinking hidden | use pseudocode/flowcharts |
| criteria not tested later | evaluation unsupported | test each criterion |
| non-functional criteria too subjective | hard to judge | make them observable/measurable |

